A split makes both halves the lower one,
so the two claim the same memory, and the upper half is nobody's.
The upper half goes right after the Untyped it was split from, before the lower one, and covers it,
so the list reads the lower half as made of the upper, and the prologue's split of the upper half is refused.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-smp2 fuzz-work test qemu-replay
Report: a fresh kernel refused prologue record N with N

diff --git a/kernel/syscall.c b/kernel/syscall.c
--- a/kernel/syscall.c
+++ b/kernel/syscall.c
@@ -320,7 +320,7 @@ static int op_untyped(struct thread *t, uint32_t slot, const struct cap *cap,
         }
         /* Both slots are free and distinct, so neither store fails and the split is whole or not at all. */
         struct cap lower = cap_to_untyped(base, half, cap->rights);
-        struct cap upper = cap_to_untyped(base + half, half, cap->rights);
+        struct cap upper = cap_to_untyped(base, half, cap->rights);
         lower.orig = 1;
         upper.orig = 1;
         slot_store(t, arg[1], &lower, u);
